 ## Operating characteristics
 
-**Time Complexity:**
-*   Preprocessing: $O(N^3 \log N)$ for the 3D FFT [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Per-View Rendering: $O(M^2 \log M)$ for the 2D inverse FFT of the extracted slice, where $M$ is the image resolution [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Overall: Asymptotically less expensive than conventional volume rendering ($O(N^3)$ compositing), but has a high constant factor [S2, unofficial].
-
-**Memory Complexity:**
-*   Varies from 48 MB to 256 MB for a $128^3$ volume, depending on the shading model complexity (e.g., equation 3.1.4 vs 3.3.2) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Using an FHT (Fast Hartley Transform) instead of FFT halves memory requirements [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Tested on a 36 MHz Silicon Graphics 4D/320 VGX using a sequential C implementation [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Per-view rendering time varied from 6 seconds (simple shading) to over a minute (complex shading, due mostly to paging) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Optimizing the filter and trimming zero padding can provide another factor of two speedup [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths:**
-*   Fast generation of attenuation-only images (X-ray-like) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Amenable to hardware acceleration using video coprocessors or DSPs [S1, unofficial].
-*   Can be parallelized at fine grain (within FFT) or coarse grain (between slices) [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Limitations:**
-*   High constant factor makes it slower than conventional methods for very small volumes [S2, unofficial].
-*   Hardwired preprocessing (edge enhancement/windowing) hinders interactive segmentation [S1, unofficial].
-*   Ghosting artifacts if zero padding is insufficient [S1, S2, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   When generating attenuation-only projections or X-ray-like images is the primary goal.
-*   When the volume is large enough that the $O(N^2 \log N)$ per-view cost is advantageous over $O(N^3)$ compositing.
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   When interactive segmentation with dynamic preprocessing is required.
-*   For very small volumes where the overhead of FFT and interpolation outweighs the benefits.
+### Time and Memory Complexity
+*   **Preprocessing:** $O(N^3 \log N)$ for the 3D FFT, where $N$ is the volume dimension.
+*   **Per-View Rendering:** $O(R^2 \log R)$ for the 2D IFFT of the extracted slice, where $R$ is the image resolution. The slice extraction itself is $O(R^2)$ with tricubic interpolation.
+*   **Memory:** Varies based on the shading model. In the original implementation, memory requirements ranged from 48 MB to 256 MB for a $128^3$ volume [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+### Measured Performance
+*   **Hardware:** Tested on a 36 MHz Silicon Graphics 4D/320 VGX using a sequential C implementation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Per-View Time:** Varied from 6 seconds (for simpler shading models) to over a minute (for more complex models, due mostly to paging) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **GPU Acceleration:** A CUDA-based implementation achieved a 117x speed-up compared to a single-threaded hybrid CPU/GPU implementation [S4, unofficial].
+
+### Scaling
+*   The algorithm is asymptotically less expensive than conventional volume rendering for very large volumes, but the high constant factor makes it slower for smaller volumes [S2, unofficial].
+*   Parallelization can be achieved at a fine grain (within the FFT) or a coarse grain (between slices) for greater speedups [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Strengths and Limitations
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient computation of projections using the Fourier projection-slice theorem; ability to incorporate shading models in the frequency domain; potential for hardware acceleration [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory requirements for large volumes; sensitivity to preprocessing changes (requiring full spectrum recomputation); potential for ghosting artifacts if zero-padding is insufficient [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+### When to Use
+*   Use when rendering large volumes where the cost of traditional ray-casting is prohibitive.
+*   Use when attenuation-only or simple shading models are sufficient.
+*   Use when hardware acceleration (GPU) is available.
+
+### When Not to Use
+*   Use when interactive data segmentation with frequent changes to edge enhancement/windowing is required, as this necessitates full spectrum recomputation [S1, unofficial].
+*   Use when very small volumes are rendered, as the overhead may outweigh the benefits [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
 
-**Origin:**
